00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:28.500
Wow.

266
00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:30.520
Oh, thank you.

267
00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:31.620
Oh, my gosh.

268
00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:32.500
That was so fun.

269
00:10:32.580 --> 00:10:35.760
Well, that, I know it wasn't a fast fact, but that was pretty great.

270
00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:36.780
Yeah.

271
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:39.220
That was like an Office Ladies big fact.

272
00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:39.820
Yeah.

273
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:44.520
Well, I thought that since it's our birthday, in lieu of fast facts,

274
00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.520
I thought we could just go to our mailbag and answer some frequently asked questions

275
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:50.140
this week.

276
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:51.320
Oh, I love that.

277
00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:55.420
Here is a fan question from Eve and Maria Barbic.

278
00:10:55.620 --> 00:11:00.720
They said, each time there is a conference room meeting, is everyone given an assigned seat

279
00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.080
or can you sit where you want?

280
00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:04.960
We were always given an assigned seat.

281
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:09.240
We would walk into the conference room and they would usually have a little sticky note

282
00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:10.800
on each chair with our name on it.

283
00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:11.100
Yeah.

284
00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.980
And we knew that was our spot because they would plan it out.

285
00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:19.140
They would plan the camera blocking and the camera angles and how they were going to catch

286
00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:23.140
all of our reactions according to who had dialogue and who didn't.

287
00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:23.700
Yeah.

288
00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:27.020
So they planned all that out before we even walked in the room.

289
00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:27.400
Yeah.

290
00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:32.880
Sometimes someone's dialogue would affect someone else and you would need to see their reaction

291
00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.600
immediately after the line of dialogue.

292
00:11:35.720 --> 00:11:37.880
So they had that all mapped out.

293
00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:43.680
And sometimes if you were really big and pregnant, you were in the back row, just going to say.

294
00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:45.280
Yes.

295
00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.960
So we could hide that big old belly.

296
00:11:47.980 --> 00:11:48.460
That's right.

297
00:11:48.980 --> 00:11:50.860
We're getting close to that, Ange.

298
00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:52.000
To my belly?

299
00:11:52.600 --> 00:11:53.040
Yeah.

300
00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:53.640
I know.

301
00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:54.440
That's next season.

302
00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:55.020
I know.

303
00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:56.860
That's going to be crazy for me.

304
00:11:57.040 --> 00:11:58.700
I can't wait to hear those stories.

305
00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:01.000
I'm just going to probably drive you guys all crazy.

306
00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:05.220
So I'm going to be like, in this one, Isabel was really kicking.

307
00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:07.620
I can't wait.

308
00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:08.980
I can't wait.

309
00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:10.340
We're going to get it all down on record.

310
00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:11.380
What else you got?

311
00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:12.660
All right.

312
00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.940
This question comes from Raisha Patterson.

313
00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:21.840
Can you explain the decision of the audience not hearing the questions posed by the documentary

314
00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:22.300
crew?

315
00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.900
So like in our talking heads, right?

316
00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:25.140
Yeah.

317
00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:31.600
She asked, did Greg Daniels make this decision or was this taken from the BBC version?

318
00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:33.840
Well, I mean, I have my thoughts.

319
00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:40.040
My thoughts are usually in a documentary, the camera crew wants to disappear.

320
00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:45.920
They want to really capture people's lives and they try not to like interfere too much.

321
00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.400
And so I would guess, and I think you probably have the answer, that they wanted it that way.

322
00:12:52.680 --> 00:12:54.800
They wanted it to really feel like a documentary.

323
00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:56.520
Yeah.

324
00:12:56.780 --> 00:12:59.700
I reached out to Greg, Ange, and you are exactly right.

325
00:12:59.700 --> 00:13:05.800
He said they made the decision because he felt like the audience would be more invested

326
00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:10.540
and more connected to our characters if you didn't hear anyone asking the questions.

327
00:13:10.540 --> 00:13:16.100
If your mind wasn't thinking about this crew of people filming them.

328
00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:16.700
Yeah.

329
00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:19.860
But he said they did wonder about this all the time.

330
00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:25.740
They wondered about what are the circumstances when they might reveal someone from the crew.

331
00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:31.840
And I remember, at least on set, Angela, I don't know if you remember this, but there was this

332
00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.400
big debate about in the final season or ever.

333
00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:41.700
If we reveal who the documentarian is, who's been making this, who should it be?

334
00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.300
And I remember the cast saying that it should either be Greg Daniels or Ken Kwapis.

335
00:13:47.480 --> 00:13:48.860
Do you remember this conversation?

336
00:13:48.860 --> 00:13:49.440
I do.

337
00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:50.380
I do.

338
00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:52.620
And I remember I wanted that.

339
00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:54.460
I wanted to see the camera crew.

340
00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:59.820
But also, Jenna, I don't know what it's like for you when you watch actual documentaries.

341
00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:00.760
I love them.

342
00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:09.620
And if I ever hear the voice of the documentarian off camera, I'm instantly fascinated.

343
00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:11.980
I'm like, okay, I want to see this person.

344
00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:12.880
Yeah.

345
00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:14.280
You want to see a shot of their face.

346
00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:14.580
Yeah.

347
00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:14.880
Right?

348
00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:16.820
You're like, I've heard a voice.

349
00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:17.120
Yeah.

350
00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:21.940
Well, Greg told me that there was also some debate that maybe it should be Randall Einhorn

351
00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:23.080
or Ricky Gervais.

352
00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:23.920
Oh.

353
00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:29.780
That, like, at the very end, it would be revealed that Ricky Gervais was our documentarian.

354
00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:34.460
I would like to go one step further and say, what if it was David Brent?

355
00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:36.360
Oh, no.

356
00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:41.820
What if David Brent had come from the UK to make a documentary in America?

357
00:14:42.360 --> 00:14:43.300
Like his own.

358
00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:49.420
Well, I guess there was a problem with this idea because Greg told me that there are a

359
00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:52.220
few times that you do hear the voice for the documentarian.

360
00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:53.700
And do you know whose voice it is?

361
00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:54.860
Ken Kwapis?

362
00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:56.920
Dave Rogers.

363
00:14:57.440 --> 00:14:58.660
Oh, no way.

364
00:14:59.200 --> 00:14:59.680
Right?

365
00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:04.340
And when Greg told me that, I couldn't even think of any times that we heard the voice.

366
00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:05.840
Did he loop it in?

367
00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:06.860
Like, when did he?

368
00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:07.320
Yeah.

369
00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:09.820
And I don't know why or when.

370
00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.000
So, we haven't gotten to it, right?

371
00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.840
Like, we haven't heard a documentarian yet so far.

372
00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:16.200
Yeah.

373
00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:17.380
So, it must be coming up.

374
00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:18.540
We're going to have to track it.

375
00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:19.200
Oh, wow.

376
00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:20.260
I want to track that.

377
00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:21.320
These are good.

378
00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.340
These are good questions.

379
00:15:22.780 --> 00:15:23.880
I thought so, too.

380
00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:24.280
All right.

381
00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:25.400
Are you ready for another one?

382
00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:25.700
Yeah.

383
00:15:26.340 --> 00:15:26.860
All right.

384
00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.120
This question we got a lot.

385
00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:29.640
Oh.

386
00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.960
This was definitely a most frequently asked.

387
00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:40.600
We got this question from Julianne Kay, Luke D, Cade Smith, Alana Boylan, and two Emilies.

388
00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:41.240
Okay.

389
00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:47.000
The first one is Emily Howard, and the second is my own sister, Emily.

390
00:15:47.180 --> 00:15:48.680
You texted me this question.

391
00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:55.600
She has been catching up on our podcast, Angela, and it's so funny because I'll just suddenly

392
00:15:55.600 --> 00:16:00.200
get a text where she's like, oh, my God, you told people I threw up in Ellie's bushes?

393
00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:00.920
I know.

394
00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:01.980
I know.

395
00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:02.780
I know.

396
00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:05.060
And I'm like, oh, yeah, you're just catching that.

397
00:16:05.420 --> 00:16:05.560
Yeah.

398
00:16:05.740 --> 00:16:06.920
That happened a while ago.

399
00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:07.500
Yeah.

400
00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:10.640
My sister, Janet, listens, and so does my stepson, Jack.

401
00:16:11.760 --> 00:16:14.140
And remember when we did Laundry and Betrayal?

402
00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:15.200
Yes.

403
00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.900
Jack came out of his bedroom with his headphones on and was like, Angela!

404
00:16:21.180 --> 00:16:22.520
I was like, oh, sorry.

405
00:16:22.820 --> 00:16:24.740
Our kids don't hear us talk like that.

406
00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:26.080
What did Emily ask?

407
00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:26.680
Okay.

408
00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:32.300
They all want to know, why do we have multiple directors on the show instead of just one?

409
00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.340
So he can prep the other one, right?

410
00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:34.800
I mean.

411
00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:38.520
So I actually called my sister because I was like, I don't know how to put this in a text.

412
00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:40.080
And I explained it like this.

413
00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:48.440
I said, you know, it takes us one week to shoot an episode, but the director works the week prior

414
00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:51.040
preparing it and the week after editing it.

415
00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:53.440
So the director's job is three weeks long.

416
00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:57.400
So you couldn't have someone direct every single episode.

417
00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.880
It would just be impossible.

418
00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:03.640
But she was like, well, I don't know, because sometimes I watch these streaming shows and

419
00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:05.240
they have the same director the whole time.

420
00:17:05.420 --> 00:17:10.320
And I'm like, yeah, that's because on those streaming shows, a lot of times they've written.

421
00:17:10.460 --> 00:17:12.060
It's a short number of scripts.

422
00:17:12.060 --> 00:17:13.880
It's six scripts or eight scripts.

423
00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.120
And they write them all.

424
00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:17.980
And then they kind of shoot them like one big long movie.

425
00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:18.500
That's true.

426
00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.800
And they do all their prep beforehand and they do all their editing after.

427
00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:24.700
And they'll shoot out of order, too.

428
00:17:24.840 --> 00:17:28.100
They'll, like a movie, they will wrap out locations.

429
00:17:28.100 --> 00:17:33.040
So it'll be like, we're going to shoot every single scene we have at the high school this

430
00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:33.340
week.

431
00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:33.540
Yeah.

432
00:17:33.700 --> 00:17:36.100
And next week we're going to shoot every scene at the house.

433
00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:38.340
And they shoot like a movie.

434
00:17:39.060 --> 00:17:44.060
But on our show, we shot each episode like its own little mini movie.

435
00:17:45.160 --> 00:17:46.580
So that's why.

436
00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:50.380
And we also did like 24 instead of six.

437
00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:51.640
Exactly.

438
00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:55.140
And they would be writing while we were shooting.

439
00:17:55.660 --> 00:18:00.880
So, yeah, that's why we have one director per episode and they switch.

440
00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:01.280
Yeah.

441
00:18:01.640 --> 00:18:03.700
So now, Angela, this one comes to you.

442
00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.400
This is from Christina Kuzland.

443
00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:06.800
Okay.

444
00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:13.940
And she said this, Angela, can you say something in Indonesian on your podcast?

445
00:18:14.180 --> 00:18:17.400
Because my daughter, Alana, is studying the language in school.

446
00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:22.340
She is a big fan of the show and thought it was so cool that the person who played her

447
00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:25.500
favorite office character had grown up in Indonesia.

448
00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:26.220
All right.

449
00:18:26.380 --> 00:18:29.320
Alani, how about we say, hi, how are you?

450
00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:29.960
I'm fine.

451
00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:30.480
Thank you.

452
00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:31.880
Okay, Jenna, we'll do it together.

453
00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:32.580
Yes.

454
00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:35.180
So, how are you is apakabar.

455
00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:36.700
Apakabar.

456
00:18:36.800 --> 00:18:37.440
Apakabai.

457
00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:38.180
Terima kasih.

458
00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:39.600
I love it.

459
00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:40.720
There you go.

460
00:18:40.820 --> 00:18:42.720
That's just a little simple phrase.

461
00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:45.500
But I mean, I loved growing up in Indonesia.

462
00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:47.880
I had such a wonderful childhood there.

463
00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:49.560
And I have very fond memories.

464
00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:51.600
So, thank you for asking that question.

465
00:18:51.760 --> 00:18:56.280
I get really excited if I ever run into anyone that can speak Indonesian.

466
00:18:56.280 --> 00:19:05.200
Like, years ago, I was at the mall, and there was a couple over by a bunch of shoes, and they

467
00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:06.200
were talking about the shoes.

468
00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:09.260
And the man said, Wadu Mahal Sakali.

469
00:19:10.280 --> 00:19:12.920
And what he was saying was like, these are really expensive.

470
00:19:13.540 --> 00:19:14.660
And I got so excited.

471
00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:15.200
And I was like, ah!

472
00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:16.740
And the vagina bahasa Indonesia!

473
00:19:17.120 --> 00:19:18.500
Like, I was like, you guys speak Indonesian?

474
00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:19.880
And they looked at me like, what?

475
00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:23.060
And then we spoke Indonesian.

476
00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:24.960
I really geek out.

477
00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:25.800
I get really excited.

478
00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:28.280
Angela, you speak three languages.

479
00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.340
Because you also speak Spanish.

480
00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:31.680
Un poco.

481
00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:36.080
Yo, yo entiendo más que hablar, ¿sí?

482
00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:38.980
I feel like I understand it better than I can speak it.

483
00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:40.020
Yeah.

484
00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:42.520
But I really admire this about you.

485
00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:45.520
I wish that I spoke another language fluently, and I don't.

486
00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:49.680
Well, you know, during this time, I've been doing Duolingo.

487
00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.120
I love Duolingo.

488
00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:52.340
Which language?

489
00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:54.320
I've been just brushing up on Spanish.

490
00:19:54.500 --> 00:19:58.760
Because I feel like I could be close, you know, closer to being fluent in Spanish.

491
00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:02.100
And Indonesian is great, but I don't get a lot of chance to use it here.

492
00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:03.200
Yeah.

493
00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:03.840
Yeah.

494
00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:05.680
I don't get a lot of opportunities to speak it.

495
00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.120
Well, thank you to Christina and Alani.

496
00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:10.560
We really liked that question.

497
00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:11.220
Thanks, guys.

498
00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:12.000
Trima kasih.

499
00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:16.540
So, and you know that we get a lot of mail from people telling us how much The Office

500
00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:20.140
and this podcast is helping to get them through tough times.

501
00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:20.920
Yeah.

502
